{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HL44Jrae7biAwojwtdLShx","type":"tool_result","content":"47:**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n57:**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n405:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n421:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n427:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n432:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n438:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n443:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n450:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n456:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n461:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n465:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`\n467:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n474:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n480:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n485:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n493:**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The cl\n498:**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, {\n500:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (\n506:**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n512:**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n516:**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n535:**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed ste\n541:**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1\n548:**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island\n555:**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n562:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turn\n564:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with hei\n566:**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on a\n568:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n573:**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the part\n575:**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n579:**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n584:**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n588:**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own spe\n590:**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate whe\n592:**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` \n594:**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })\n596:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera r\n598:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil a\n600:**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n634:**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole \n640:**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two \n642:**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\\n644:**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complet\n646:**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174837__x7ac903/work","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"8473b268-4355-4549-8de0-13c0bf76a59f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:50:18.817Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"47:**Why games look cheap, and the fix for each:**\n57:**Art recipes by classic** (combine with the genre recipes below):\n405:**Side-scroller (Mario, Mega Man, Castlevania).**\n421:**Metroidvania (Metroid, Super Metroid, Symphony of the Night).** Start from the METROIDVANIA slice (the rooms module).\n427:**Zelda-like adventure.**\n432:**Beat-'em-up (Final Fight, Streets of Rage).** Start from the BRAWLER slice.\n438:**Fighting game (Street Fighter II, Darkstalkers).** Start from the FIGHTING slice (the fight module).\n443:**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n450:**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n456:**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n461:**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n465:**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`\n467:**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n474:**Puzzle (Tetris, Dr. Mario, Puyo Puyo).** Start from the PUZZLE slice.\n480:**Roguelike (Rogue, Mystery Dungeon, Shiren).** Start from the ROGUELIKE slice.\n485:**Tactics (Final Fantasy Tactics, Tactics Ogre, Fire Emblem).** Start from the TACTICS slice (the grid and story modules).\n493:**Point-and-click adventure (Monkey Island, Day of the Tentacle, Broken Sword).** Start from the POINT-AND-CLICK slice (the click and story modules). The cl\n498:**Visual novel (Phoenix Wright, Snatcher, Clannad).** A painted backdrop per scene (`r.overlay`), the speaker's rig large (`rig.drawPortrait(g, x, y, 120, {\n500:**Real-time strategy (Dune II, Warcraft II, Command & Conquer, StarCraft).** Start from the RTS slice (the grid and strategy modules). The strategy module (\n506:**Theme park or city sim (Theme Park, RollerCoaster Tycoon, SimCity).** Start from the SIM slice (the grid and strategy modules).\n512:**3D-era remake (Mario 64, Ocarina, Crash).**\n516:**Third-person stealth and parkour (Assassin's Creed, Metal Gear, Tenchu).** Start from the STEALTH slice.\n535:**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed ste\n541:**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1\n548:**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island\n555:**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n562:**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turn\n564:**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with hei\n566:**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on a\n568:**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n573:**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the part\n575:**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n579:**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n584:**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n588:**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own spe\n590:**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate whe\n592:**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` \n594:**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })\n596:**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera r\n598:**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil a\n600:**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n634:**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole \n640:**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two \n642:**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\\n644:**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complet\n646:**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174837__x7ac903/work","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01HL44Jrae7biAwojwtdLShx","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyDoGSMu7HTiepHZuob","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KnoPo6UkSeiXCxbDneyKYj","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","offset":443,"limit":30},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2890,"cache_read_input_tokens":92410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2890},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"b75a9e3d-7e6a-454b-bb51-d09553d04e78","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:50:20.924Z","request_id":"req_011CggyDo2J2qJ3163vA8SW5","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KnoPo6UkSeiXCxbDneyKYj":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","offset":443,"limit":30}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KnoPo6UkSeiXCxbDneyKYj","type":"tool_result","content":"443\t**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n444\t- Use `view: 'overhead'` so world x/y equal screen pixels; `res: [240, 320]` for a vertical stage, `[320, 240]` for a horizontal one, with `expand`.\n445\t- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n446\t- Flyers keep up with the camera: each step add the scroll to their position (and to the bullets'), while the ground and the rock stay put.\n447\t- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n448\t- Bosses fire `E.pattern.ring` and `spread`, each attack telegraphed. A bomb clears `shots.clear('foe')`. The end: `E.Arcade`'s continue, then initials and the table (the arcade module).\n449\t\n450\t**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n451\t- One fixed screen (`res: [232, 270]` or `[320, 240]` with `expand`): centre the playfield and fill the rest with cabinet art drawn by code, or let the field be the whole screen (the rocks wrap at its edges).\n452\t- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.update(dt)` each step and `cab.draw(r, box)` after the game.\n453\t- A maze is an `E.Grid` with `E.GridMover`s: the player's turn asked early is kept (`{ keep: true }`); a chaser picks, at each cell, the open way nearest its own target (never straight back). Moths fly in on `E.Path`s and turn with `r.sprite`'s `rot`; rocks drift with `E.wrap` and draw at every edge they cross.\n454\t- Each game's demo is its own bot, playing the real rules; a remaster (light, rigs, glow) with the classic look one key and one setting away.\n455\t\n456\t**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n457\t- The track is a TileMap: its walls bounce the karts, its floor tags (`grass`, `sand`, `boost`, ...) slow or boost them (`E.Car`'s `surfaces`), and `E.Mode7({ floor: (x, y) => [r, g, b] })` draws it from the same cells. The racing line is a closed `E.Path` through the track's middle: `E.Race({ path, laps: 3 })` counts laps through its checkpoints in order (a shortcut counts nothing), gives places, and drives every car not a player's (`race.add(car, { input })` for a player, `{ ai: { skill } }` for the rest).\n458\t- Each step `race.update(dt, map)` (it moves every car), then the camera behind yours, turning after it more slowly than the kart turns (a drift shows): `m7.cam = cam; m7.chase({ x, y, angle: camAngle }, { back: 42, h: 24 })`. Draw the karts with `m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => ...)`: a few boxes through `m7.view(k)`'s projection and the rig seated (`down: .4`) in the middle, sorted far to near from the camera's side.\n459\t- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i])`. On a phone the gas goes on by itself (`car.o.auto`), the stick steers, DRIFT and ITEM are buttons; held upright, draw from further back with a wider field of view.\n460\t\n461\t**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n462\t- `E.Road` is the course: `road.add(n, { curve, hill, tunnel, colors })` stretch by stretch; scenery with `road.place(z, offset, sprite, scale)` (`E.prop('palm').frames[0]` works), traffic as `cars: [{ z, x, w, draw }]` passed to `road.draw`. The player is a position (`z` along, `x` across in half roads) and a speed, not a body.\n463\t- Each step: `x -= road.curveAt(z) * speed² * k` throws the car outward on a bend (make the hardest faster than the player can steer: they slow down), off the road (`|x| > 1`) it slows, `road.hits(z, x, w)` crashes it into the scenery (put it back on the road), and a car close in `z` and `x` bumps it. A clock that counts down and checkpoints that add time make the race.\n464\t\n465\t**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n466\t\n467\t**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n468\t- The field is a scene of `TileMap`s: a town, a world map (past 128 x 128 cells its floor bakes in chunks: paint woods and peaks into `floorTex`, block them), dungeons; a map's floor tag leads to another (an exit, a cave's mouth). Walk a `Body` with the stick; the other members follow the leader's trail.\n469\t- People talk through an `E.Story`: their lines as data, with choices, flags, quests, emote balloons and events for the game's scripts (a shop's window, the inn's night, a boss's fight; `wait: true` holds the talk until `story.resume()`).\n470\t- The party is an `E.RPG` party: members with stats, levels and gear, a bag, gold; shops, items and `reward(troop)` after a fight. Encounters by zone (`rpg.encounters`) as the party walks: a swirl (`game.go(battle, data, { fade: 'swirl', at })`) into the battle scene, which reads the members' `stat()` and writes their hp back.\n471\t- Battles are a scene in the `brawler` view with rigs lined up, a `Menu` of commands and a queue of actions (the RPG slice's ATB battle). Each action steps forward, swings, applies damage and steps back.\n472\t- Saves: `game.saves` (the party's `state()`, the story's `state()`, the map and the place); a crystal to save at, the inn's autosave, Continue and Load on the title. The menu's windows are `rpg.list` and `rpg.card`, rows a thumb's height on a phone."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"7c337e48-9852-435b-bc47-bfcc4d5166a9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:50:20.933Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","content":"**Shoot-'em-up (1942, Gradius).** Start from the SHOOTER slice: its first stage is vertical, its second horizontal.\n- Use `view: 'overhead'` so world x/y equal screen pixels; `res: [240, 320]` for a vertical stage, `[320, 240]` for a horizontal one, with `expand`.\n- Ships are sprites or vector parts drawn by code; a timeline array spawns waves by time or by how far the camera has come; enemies move on formulas (sine lines, dives aimed with `E.pattern.aim`) or along `E.Path`s.\n- Flyers keep up with the camera: each step add the scroll to their position (and to the bullets'), while the ground and the rock stay put.\n- Horizontal stages (Gradius, R-Type): the rock above and below is two functions of x (`highY(x)`, `lowY(x)`) drawn in columns and tested for crashes; a power bar is a cursor that capsules move along, a button taking the lit slot; options follow the ship's past path (a ring of its positions, an option every 12 behind); a mid-boss loops a closed `E.Path` while the scroll waits.\n- Bosses fire `E.pattern.ring` and `spread`, each attack telegraphed. A bomb clears `shots.clear('foe')`. The end: `E.Arcade`'s continue, then initials and the table (the arcade module).\n\n**Golden-age arcade (Pac-Man, Galaga, Asteroids).** Start from the ARCADE slice: a cabinet of three.\n- One fixed screen (`res: [232, 270]` or `[320, 240]` with `expand`): centre the playfield and fill the rest with cabinet art drawn by code, or let the field be the whole screen (the rocks wrap at its edges).\n- `new E.Arcade(game, { id, lives: 3, extra: [10000] })` keeps the score, lives, two players' turns (each one's stage in `p.save`), the continue, the tables with initials and the attract mode; `cab.update(dt)` each step and `cab.draw(r, box)` after the game.\n- A maze is an `E.Grid` with `E.GridMover`s: the player's turn asked early is kept (`{ keep: true }`); a chaser picks, at each cell, the open way nearest its own target (never straight back). Moths fly in on `E.Path`s and turn with `r.sprite`'s `rot`; rocks drift with `E.wrap` and draw at every edge they cross.\n- Each game's demo is its own bot, playing the real rules; a remaster (light, rigs, glow) with the classic look one key and one setting away.\n\n**Kart racing (Super Mario Kart, F-Zero).** Start from the KART slice (the mode7 and drive modules).\n- The track is a TileMap: its walls bounce the karts, its floor tags (`grass`, `sand`, `boost`, ...) slow or boost them (`E.Car`'s `surfaces`), and `E.Mode7({ floor: (x, y) => [r, g, b] })` draws it from the same cells. The racing line is a closed `E.Path` through the track's middle: `E.Race({ path, laps: 3 })` counts laps through its checkpoints in order (a shortcut counts nothing), gives places, and drives every car not a player's (`race.add(car, { input })` for a player, `{ ai: { skill } }` for the rest).\n- Each step `race.update(dt, map)` (it moves every car), then the camera behind yours, turning after it more slowly than the kart turns (a drift shows): `m7.cam = cam; m7.chase({ x, y, angle: camAngle }, { back: 42, h: 24 })`. Draw the karts with `m7.add(x, y, z, (g, sx, sy, k) => ...)`: a few boxes through `m7.view(k)`'s projection and the rig seated (`down: .4`) in the middle, sorted far to near from the camera's side.\n- Two players: `game.allowJoin({ max: 2 })`, `game.splitScreen(2, { layout: 'rows' })`, and in `draw` each viewport its own camera: `m7.draw(r, cams[r.viewport.i])`, `race.hud(r, players[r.viewport.i])`. On a phone the gas goes on by itself (`car.o.auto`), the stick steers, DRIFT and ITEM are buttons; held upright, draw from further back with a wider field of view.\n\n**Road racing (OutRun, Pole Position, Hang-On).** Start from the ROAD slice (the road module).\n- `E.Road` is the course: `road.add(n, { curve, hill, tunnel, colors })` stretch by stretch; scenery with `road.place(z, offset, sprite, scale)` (`E.prop('palm').frames[0]` works), traffic as `cars: [{ z, x, w, draw }]` passed to `road.draw`. The player is a position (`z` along, `x` across in half roads) and a speed, not a body.\n- Each step: `x -= road.curveAt(z) * speed² * k` throws the car outward on a bend (make the hardest faster than the player can steer: they slow down), off the road (`|x| > 1`) it slows, `road.hits(z, x, w)` crashes it into the scenery (put it back on the road), and a car close in `z` and `x` bumps it. A clock that counts down and checkpoints that add time make the race.\n\n**Top-down racing and driving (Micro Machines, RC Pro-Am, GTA).** `E.Car` on a TileMap in the `topdown` view: the same cars, laps and drivers as the kart (`E.Race`), drawn from above (a box of `px.poly` on `r.w()` corner points, turned by `car.angle`); GTA's car is a `E.Car` the hero gets into (`kart: false`: drift is a handbrake).\n\n**JRPG (Dragon Quest, Final Fantasy).** Start from the RPG slice (the story and rpg modules).\n- The field is a scene of `TileMap`s: a town, a world map (past 128 x 128 cells its floor bakes in chunks: paint woods and peaks into `floorTex`, block them), dungeons; a map's floor tag leads to another (an exit, a cave's mouth). Walk a `Body` with the stick; the other members follow the leader's trail.\n- People talk through an `E.Story`: their lines as data, with choices, flags, quests, emote balloons and events for the game's scripts (a shop's window, the inn's night, a boss's fight; `wait: true` holds the talk until `story.resume()`).\n- The party is an `E.RPG` party: members with stats, levels and gear, a bag, gold; shops, items and `reward(troop)` after a fight. Encounters by zone (`rpg.encounters`) as the party walks: a swirl (`game.go(battle, data, { fade: 'swirl', at })`) into the battle scene, which reads the members' `stat()` and writes their hp back.\n- Battles are a scene in the `brawler` view with rigs lined up, a `Menu` of commands and a queue of actions (the RPG slice's ATB battle). Each action steps forward, swings, applies damage and steps back.\n- Saves: `game.saves` (the party's `state()`, the story's `state()`, the map and the place); a crystal to save at, the inn's autosave, Continue and Load on the title. The menu's windows are `rpg.list` and `rpg.card`, rows a thumb's height on a phone.","numLines":30,"startLine":443,"totalLines":665}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KnoPo6UkSeiXCxbDneyKYj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggyDoGSMu7HTiepHZuob","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KNva93V9GX3ugyPCYghfmY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","offset":535,"limit":125},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2890,"cache_read_input_tokens":92410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2890},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"7cc73ecd-62f9-4219-917a-049d84d6b5ca","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:50:21.906Z","request_id":"req_011CggyDo2J2qJ3163vA8SW5","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KNva93V9GX3ugyPCYghfmY":{"file_path":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","offset":535,"limit":125}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KNva93V9GX3ugyPCYghfmY","type":"tool_result","content":"535\t**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n536\t- Breakout: a closed `world.chain` for the walls (its floor a sensor that loses the ball), the bricks static boxes taken out on `on.hit` (`world.remove`), the paddle a kinematic box moved by `moveTo` (it gives the ball its speed), the ball a `bullet` circle with `bounce: 1, friction: 0` and a `maxSpeed`; nudge its angle away from flat on each paddle hit.\n537\t- Asteroids with real collisions: rocks as dynamic convex `world.poly`s with a little `spin`, the ship a poly with `gravity: 0` and `damping`, wrap-around by moving bodies across the edges yourself (`E.wrap`); a rock that splits is removed and two smaller polys added at its place with its speed.\n538\t- Lemmings: an `E.Terrain` level (a picture's cells as materials), each lemming a walker (`land.walk`: a step up of 5, a fall that kills past a landing speed), diggers and bashers carving a small circle or a polygon each step, builders adding a short bar (`addPoly`); hundreds of walkers are cheap (no physics bodies).\n539\t- What touches the terrain fast (a shot) uses `land.ray` for its line; what bounces on it (a grenade) is a physics circle with `world.add(land.collider())`.\n540\t\n541\t**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n542\t- The walls `world.chain`s with `r: 1.5`; the flippers `world.flipper` (flip on the keys' down and up: `flip(input.down('flipL'))`), bumpers and slingshots with a `kick`, a saucer and a kickback as `world.kicker`s, drop targets static boxes set `off` when hit, lanes and the spinner sensors (`on.enter`); the ball a `bullet` with a `maxSpeed`.\n543\t- A ramp: rails on a layer of their own; a sensor at its mouth switches a climbing ball to that layer (`ball.layer = ball.mask = 2`), one at its end switches it back; draw the ball at the ramp's height there.\n544\t- The rules are counters over the events: targets light the ramp, ramps light the jackpot, the saucer pays it and starts a multiball (more balls in the world); a ball saver's seconds after a launch; a nudge pushes every ball and fills a tilt meter that, full, kills the flippers for the ball.\n545\t- A dot-matrix display: draw the words small on a 128 x 32 canvas, then each of its pixels as a dot (lit or dim). Paint the playfield once to a canvas and draw it each frame.\n546\t- On a phone: `input.zones = [{ x0: 0, y0: 0, x1: .5, y1: 1, act: 'flipL' }, { x0: .5, y0: 0, x1: 1, y1: 1, act: 'flipR' }]` (each half holds its flipper, two thumbs at once); while the ball waits in the lane, the whole screen one zone for the plunger, a swipe up (`input.pan()`) its pull.\n547\t\n548\t**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n549\t- Soldiers are walkers (`land.walk`): its landing speed past a limit is fall damage; under the water line they drown. A blast `land.carve`s, hurts each one by its distance and throws it (`vx`, `vy`, `ground = false`).\n550\t- Turns: `new E.Turns(teams, { mode: 'speed' })` with the two teams as its actors (equal speeds alternate them); each team sends its next one still standing.\n551\t- Shots: a rocket steps its own flight (gravity, the wind, `land.ray` for the line); a grenade is a physics circle in a world of its own with `land.collider()` (bounce, a fuse in steps); a cluster bursts into a fan of small ones.\n552\t- The CPU tries its shots in simulation: for each weapon, angle and power it runs the same step the real shot will (a grenade in a fresh world), scores the blasts it would make (the foes' hurt, its own side's against it), takes the best and fires it with a little error (`Math.random`, seeded in tests). Spread the tries over steps (a few each) so the frame stays smooth.\n553\t- On a phone: `input.steer = 'drag'`; while `input.stickOn`, add up `input.drag()`: the shot goes the other way, its power the pull's length; let go fires. A tap (`clicked()`) walks there. A minimap at the top shows the whole island.\n554\t\n555\t**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n556\t- A chart is a string a lane over the song's steps (`'x...x...'`, holds `'h==='`), in parts and an order (`{ parts: { A: [...], B: [...] }, order: 'I A A B' }`), or a track's own notes (`{ track: 1, lanes: 4 }`). Give the song a `len` and let its tracks loop under it.\n557\t- `rh.start()`, then `rh.update()` each step: it judges each lane's press by its distance in ms from its note on the music clock (windows of about 45, 90 and 135 ms suit a phone), and counts combo, score, life and grade. A miss and its sound are yours (`on.judge`).\n558\t- PaRappa's call and response: the teacher's notes are calls (`'c'`), sounded on their steps (`sfx(voice, { step })`) and drawn on a highway of their own (`rh.draw(r, { call: true })`); the player answers in the next bar.\n559\t- On a phone the lanes are columns: `input.zones` of a quarter of the screen each, under a highway of four lanes a quarter of the screen apart, so a thumb plays the note above it.\n560\t- Let the player set the audio offset (Settings, Sound, Calibrate; or `audio.calibrate()` on a screen of your own) before blaming them for being late. Move the dancer, the lights and the crowd with `audio.onBeat`.\n561\t\n562\t**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n563\t\n564\t**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n565\t\n566\t**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n567\t\n568\t**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n569\t- The field is a `TileMap` cage in `threequarter` (boards that cut low before the camera, nets); the ball and the players are `E.Body`s. The ball bounces off the ground and the boards (`bounce`), and curls: turn its velocity a little across its path each step while the shooter pushes sideways (aftertouch).\n570\t- A pass leads the teammate (aim where it is going, not where it is); a lob over a defender is `E.arc(from, to, gravity, { apex })`, the throw that lands at its feet; a shot's power fills a meter while its button is held and goes on letting go.\n571\t- The players a person doesn't run play by formation, by where the ball is: with it, the other one runs ahead on the far side; without it, the nearer presses and the other covers the goal. `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` lets players 2 to 4 in, each taking a side's next player.\n572\t\n573\t**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n574\t\n575\t**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n576\t- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n577\t- Waves spawn from the room's edges by a timeline; grunts walk straight at the hero, others wander, hulks can't be killed, humans to rescue for points. `cam.shake` and `game.freeze(.03)` on every kill keep it loud; `E.Arcade` keeps the score, the lives and the table.\n578\t\n579\t**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n580\t- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n581\t- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\n582\t- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n583\t\n584\t**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n585\t- Up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors.\n586\t- Generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero (one field for all, recomputed when a hero crosses a cell), drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }` (kept images) to stay fast.\n587\t\n588\t**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n589\t\n590\t**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n591\t\n592\t**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n593\t\n594\t**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n595\t\n596\t**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n597\t\n598\t**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n599\t\n600\t**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n601\t- a platformer with Zelda rooms: `cam.room` in the side view;\n602\t- a shooter with RPG shops: scenes plus `Menu`;\n603\t- a brawler with bullet-hell bosses: `Bullets` with the `'ground'` plane;\n604\t- a puzzle game with dialog cutscenes: `Dialog`.\n605\t\n606\t## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n607\t`examples/emberdeep.html` is the engine's signature game: an endless Diablo-style hack-and-slash of about 2 MB of code, built from some fifty source files. Its structure (`src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`) scales to any large game.\n608\t\n609\t- **A spine, then content.** A small core defines:\n610\t  - the shared state;\n611\t  - one damage pipeline for every unit;\n612\t  - a handful of effect verbs (projectile, nova, ground area, telegraph, strike, meteor, chain, beam, wave, pull);\n613\t  - the hero's action model;\n614\t  - the monster runtime;\n615\t  - the level builder;\n616\t  - the UI kit.\n617\t  Content never edits the core.\n618\t- **Registries make a language.** Every part is registered: `def('skills' | 'archetypes' | 'affixes' | 'bosses' | 'patterns' | 'itemBases' | 'powers' | 'mechanics' | 'themes' | 'layouts' | 'npcs', id, spec)`. Parts refer to each other by id and combine freely. A monster is an archetype × an element × affixes × depth scaling × a palette. A level is a layout × a theme × mechanics × a monster pool. A boss is a body × an element × patterns.\n619\t- **An event bus instead of hooks everywhere.** Legendary powers, passive keystones, mechanics and tips listen to `hit`, `kill`, `skill`, `dodge`, `step` and `draw` events and check whether they apply. Adding a power never touches the combat code.\n620\t- **Procedural forever.** A recipe per depth draws from the registries. Past the hand-planned depths it composes new combinations in a fixed order (every pair of mechanics, then every triple), recolors themes by depth, varies packs (elements, shared affixes, giants, swarms) and assembles bosses from a pattern library. Both sides of the power curve grow exponentially, the monsters' a little faster.\n621\t- **New animation on the rigs.** Post-process the Humanoid's joints after `rig.update`:\n622\t  - a dodge roll turns every joint around the hips;\n623\t  - drinking re-solves one arm with `E.ik3`;\n624\t  - held tools are drawn from the hand and elbow joints.\n625\t  New creature rigs (IK-legged spiders, a path-following serpent) are posed in 3D and projected with `view.p`.\n626\t- **Test like a player.**\n627\t  - An autopilot drives the hero through a virtual input for balance runs and an attract-mode demo.\n628\t  - Scripted headless playtests (`tools/ed-play.mjs`) and a smoke test over every scene and depth (`tools/ed-smoke.mjs`) keep a big game honest.\n629\t  - A Gallery scene plays every skill and monster for visual review.\n630\t\n631\t## Where to find more: animation and examples\n632\tThe engine files carry the engine; the repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge) also holds a store of motion and a whole game of worked examples. Take from it rather than inventing a walk cycle or a spider from nothing, but take only what a game needs: the store's files are large (a clip set is 75-820 KB, each motion-capture subject about 2.5 MB), so search them and never read one whole. All of it runs at https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs.\n633\t\n634\t**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n635\t- **Find** a clip by what it does. The curated sets have catalogs, each clip's name with its tags and a description: `src/mocap/catalogs/quaternius.json` (88 clips: idles, walks, runs, jumps, sword, gun, shield, magic and fist attacks, dodges, hits, deaths, sitting, talking, work), `mesh2motion.json` (177: idles, attacks, emotes and gestures, dodges, climbing, sneaking, zombies, chores) and `cmu.json` (60 moments cut from motion capture: gaits, fights and kicks, dodges, falls and get-ups, chores, monsters and zombies acted by people). The motion-capture takes are in a ledger, one a line with its category, length and description: `node tools/cmu.mjs ledger kick` searches it, or grep `src/mocap/catalogs/cmu-takes.tsv`.\n636\t- **Take** the clips you want into a small set of your own: `node tools/anim-set.mjs src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js --clips Idle_Loop,10_01 --name MINE --out mine.js` (the curated sets are `src/mocap/sets/<set>.js`; take `NN_xx` is in `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_<NN>.js`). A clip copied by hand also needs its set's preamble (its `sources`, the bodies it was measured on), so use the tool when you can run commands.\n637\t- **Play** it: load `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js` and the set after the engine, then `const lib = Mocap.load(MOCAP.MINE); Mocap.drive(rig, lib);` once, and each step `rig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('Idle_Loop'), t)` before `rig.update`. `rig.mocapW` (0 to 1) fades a clip in and out over the rig's own animation, `rig.mocapMask = 'upper'` plays only the chest, head and arms over the rig's own walk, and `lib.moveAt(clip, t)` says how far a travelling clip has carried the body.\n638\t- **Edit** it: a clip is text. Change a key pose's numbers to fix or vary it (the Mocap Lab's AI panel shows a clip as text and plays an edit).\n639\t\n640\t**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n641\t\n642\t**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n643\t\n644\t**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n645\t\n646\t**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.\n647\t\n648\t## Performance\n649\t- **Rigs:** projection costs microseconds. The outline composite is the expensive part, so crowds should use `outline: false`.\n650\t- **Walls, floors and PlatformMap chunks** are baked once per view.\n651\t- **Canvas lighting** scans pixels inside each light, so keep radii sensible or use GPU lighting.\n652\t- **Particles** are capped at 900 (`game.particles.max`) and bullets at 800 per pool.\n653\t- **Crowd separation:** use `E.SpatialHash`, not all-pairs checks.\n654\t- The stress test (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-test) benchmarks a device and prints a copyable report.\n655\t\n656\t## Checklist before handing a game back\n657\t- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge`, holding only your game (no leftover starter menu or slices): between `GAME START` and `GAME END` in a single file, `game/game.js` in the handoff package. No libraries or network requests.\n658\t- A title scene, a gameplay scene with `pausable: true`, and a game-over or win path back to the title.\n659\t- No pause menu, settings screen, key rebinding or full-screen code of your own: they're standard. Your settings, names and menu items go in `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; prompts come from `game.input.label(action)`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"71bc8fa9-4f6b-41db-98e7-2169f9e7f70f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:50:21.916Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/.cache/plan-x7ac903/engine/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/AI_GUIDE.md","content":"**Physics toys (Breakout, Asteroids with real collisions, Lemmings).** The physics and terrain modules; each step `world.step(dt)` from the game's fixed step (never the frame's time), so a run is the same at any refresh rate.\n- Breakout: a closed `world.chain` for the walls (its floor a sensor that loses the ball), the bricks static boxes taken out on `on.hit` (`world.remove`), the paddle a kinematic box moved by `moveTo` (it gives the ball its speed), the ball a `bullet` circle with `bounce: 1, friction: 0` and a `maxSpeed`; nudge its angle away from flat on each paddle hit.\n- Asteroids with real collisions: rocks as dynamic convex `world.poly`s with a little `spin`, the ship a poly with `gravity: 0` and `damping`, wrap-around by moving bodies across the edges yourself (`E.wrap`); a rock that splits is removed and two smaller polys added at its place with its speed.\n- Lemmings: an `E.Terrain` level (a picture's cells as materials), each lemming a walker (`land.walk`: a step up of 5, a fall that kills past a landing speed), diggers and bashers carving a small circle or a polygon each step, builders adding a short bar (`addPoly`); hundreds of walkers are cheap (no physics bodies).\n- What touches the terrain fast (a shot) uses `land.ray` for its line; what bounces on it (a grenade) is a physics circle with `world.add(land.collider())`.\n\n**Pinball (Pinball Fantasies, Space Cadet).** The PINBALL kit. A view of its own looking steeply down the table (`new E.View('pinball', 'Table', 0, 62, 1, 1)`), the physics world in table units with `gravity: [0, 450]` down its slope.\n- The walls `world.chain`s with `r: 1.5`; the flippers `world.flipper` (flip on the keys' down and up: `flip(input.down('flipL'))`), bumpers and slingshots with a `kick`, a saucer and a kickback as `world.kicker`s, drop targets static boxes set `off` when hit, lanes and the spinner sensors (`on.enter`); the ball a `bullet` with a `maxSpeed`.\n- A ramp: rails on a layer of their own; a sensor at its mouth switches a climbing ball to that layer (`ball.layer = ball.mask = 2`), one at its end switches it back; draw the ball at the ramp's height there.\n- The rules are counters over the events: targets light the ramp, ramps light the jackpot, the saucer pays it and starts a multiball (more balls in the world); a ball saver's seconds after a launch; a nudge pushes every ball and fills a tilt meter that, full, kills the flippers for the ball.\n- A dot-matrix display: draw the words small on a 128 x 32 canvas, then each of its pixels as a dot (lit or dim). Paint the playfield once to a canvas and draw it each frame.\n- On a phone: `input.zones = [{ x0: 0, y0: 0, x1: .5, y1: 1, act: 'flipL' }, { x0: .5, y0: 0, x1: 1, y1: 1, act: 'flipR' }]` (each half holds its flipper, two thumbs at once); while the ball waits in the lane, the whole screen one zone for the plunger, a swipe up (`input.pan()`) its pull.\n\n**Artillery (Worms, Scorched Earth).** The ARTILLERY kit. A side view at a whole scale, so the terrain's cells stay square: `new E.View('artillery', 'Island', 0, 0, 1, 1)`; the island an `E.Terrain` (y up: draw it with `land.draw(r)`, its points at `r.w(x, 0, y)`).\n- Soldiers are walkers (`land.walk`): its landing speed past a limit is fall damage; under the water line they drown. A blast `land.carve`s, hurts each one by its distance and throws it (`vx`, `vy`, `ground = false`).\n- Turns: `new E.Turns(teams, { mode: 'speed' })` with the two teams as its actors (equal speeds alternate them); each team sends its next one still standing.\n- Shots: a rocket steps its own flight (gravity, the wind, `land.ray` for the line); a grenade is a physics circle in a world of its own with `land.collider()` (bounce, a fuse in steps); a cluster bursts into a fan of small ones.\n- The CPU tries its shots in simulation: for each weapon, angle and power it runs the same step the real shot will (a grenade in a fresh world), scores the blasts it would make (the foes' hurt, its own side's against it), takes the best and fires it with a little error (`Math.random`, seeded in tests). Spread the tries over steps (a few each) so the frame stays smooth.\n- On a phone: `input.steer = 'drag'`; while `input.stickOn`, add up `input.drag()`: the shot goes the other way, its power the pull's length; let go fires. A tap (`clicked()`) walks there. A minimap at the top shows the whole island.\n\n**Rhythm (Dance Dance Revolution, PaRappa, Beatmania).** The RHYTHM kit. The songs module's songs and the rhythm module's `E.Rhythm`.\n- A chart is a string a lane over the song's steps (`'x...x...'`, holds `'h==='`), in parts and an order (`{ parts: { A: [...], B: [...] }, order: 'I A A B' }`), or a track's own notes (`{ track: 1, lanes: 4 }`). Give the song a `len` and let its tracks loop under it.\n- `rh.start()`, then `rh.update()` each step: it judges each lane's press by its distance in ms from its note on the music clock (windows of about 45, 90 and 135 ms suit a phone), and counts combo, score, life and grade. A miss and its sound are yours (`on.judge`).\n- PaRappa's call and response: the teacher's notes are calls (`'c'`), sounded on their steps (`sfx(voice, { step })`) and drawn on a highway of their own (`rh.draw(r, { call: true })`); the player answers in the next bar.\n- On a phone the lanes are columns: `input.zones` of a quarter of the screen each, under a highway of four lanes a quarter of the screen apart, so a thumb plays the note above it.\n- Let the player set the audio offset (Settings, Sound, Calibrate; or `audio.calibrate()` on a screen of your own) before blaming them for being late. Move the dancer, the lights and the crowd with `audio.onBeat`.\n\n**Survival horror (Resident Evil, Alone in the Dark).** The 3D HORROR kit. Each room its own fixed camera (`world.camera.zones`, mode `'zones'`: fixed, turning or on a rail; the cut waits `camera.margin` into the next room, and the stick keeps its way across it), tank or modern controls (`camera.steer` by the player's `E.World3D.CONTROLS`), a pistol held to aim (`camera.lock(hero, foes)`, high and low), doors between rooms (`world.through(door, hero)`, keys with `world.unlock(door, inv)` and the rpg module's inventory), an item box and a typewriter (`trunk`, `typewriter` things; `game.saves`), a flashlight (`world.flashlight`, a world made with `{ spots: 1 }`) and fog.\n\n**3D kart racing (Mario Kart 64, Crash Team Racing).** The 3D RACING kit (the drive module). The circuit is a `world.track` along a closed `E.Path` with heights (banked bends, a hill, a hump to jump, boost `pads`, `gaps` in its walls where a shortcut, a track of its own with `surface: 'dirt'`, leaves and rejoins); karts are `world.vehicle({ kind: 'kart', rig })` raced by `E.Race` (`race.update(dt, null)`: laps, places, its drivers, items); the camera `'behind'` each; set a kart that has left the course back on the road after a moment (Lakitu); two players through `game.splitScreen(2)` and `world.addCamera`.\n\n**Rail shooter (Star Fox 64, Panzer Dragoon).** The 3D FLIGHT kit. The ship is a `world.flyer({ rail: { path, speed, w, h } })`: the rail's point rides on and the ship moves in a window across it; the camera `'behind'` follows the rail's point (`f.frame`), not the ship. Waves of foes live in the rail's frame (a distance along it and an offset across), so they keep up; a roll (`f.rolling`) turns shots aside; a held fire charges a shot that locks on (`f.seek`); at the end, `f.o.rail = null` and `bounds` give free flight round a boss. A wingman talks on `Dialog`'s radio (`modal: false`).\n\n**Sports (Sensible Soccer, NBA Jam, Tecmo Bowl).** Start from the SPORTS slice: two against two in a walled cage, up to four players.\n- The field is a `TileMap` cage in `threequarter` (boards that cut low before the camera, nets); the ball and the players are `E.Body`s. The ball bounces off the ground and the boards (`bounce`), and curls: turn its velocity a little across its path each step while the shooter pushes sideways (aftertouch).\n- A pass leads the teammate (aim where it is going, not where it is); a lob over a defender is `E.arc(from, to, gravity, { apex })`, the throw that lands at its feet; a shot's power fills a meter while its button is held and goes on letting go.\n- The players a person doesn't run play by formation, by where the ball is: with it, the other one runs ahead on the far side; without it, the nearer presses and the other covers the goal. `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })` lets players 2 to 4 in, each taking a side's next player.\n\n**First-person dungeon (Wizardry, Dungeon Master, Eye of the Beholder).** The 3D CRAWLER kit (the grid module). The 3D engine's `'fixed'` camera at the party's eyes over a level of 2 x 2 tiles a cell; each step and quarter turn is a tween, so the view glides. Gates are `world.thing('door')`s that rise (`world.unlock` for one with a key); the monsters walk an `E.Grid` cell to cell; `E.Fog` keeps what the party has seen for the automap; `E.Turns` in `'atb'` is real time (Dungeon Master), in `'speed'` the dungeon waits for the party (Wizardry). The party's portraits sit under the view (`rig.drawPortrait`).\n\n**Twin-stick shooter (Robotron 2084, Smash TV).** Start from the ADVENTURE kit (a `Body` hero in rooms) with the SHOOTER's ships and bullets.\n- One fixed room in `topdown` (`res: [320, 240]`, `expand`): the hero a `Body` moved by `input.move()`, the left stick or WASD; it fires the way the right stick points (`input.padAim`), else toward the mouse (`input.pointer()` to a ground point), else the way it moves, a shot every 0.08 s from an `E.Bullets` pool (`plane: 'ground'`).\n- Waves spawn from the room's edges by a timeline; grunts walk straight at the hero, others wander, hulks can't be killed, humans to rescue for points. `cam.shake` and `game.freeze(.03)` on every kill keep it loud; `E.Arcade` keeps the score, the lives and the table.\n\n**Bomberman.** Start from the PUZZLE slice's grid or the ARCADE's maze (the grid module).\n- The arena is an `E.Grid` of hard blocks on even cells and soft blocks to break; players and foes are `E.GridMover`s (a turn asked early is kept).\n- A bomb is a cell with a fuse (seconds in your state); its blast runs out cell by cell in four lines (`grid.line`) up to its range, stopping at a hard block, breaking the first soft one and lighting other bombs in its way (a chain). Power-ups drop from broken blocks: range, bombs, speed, a kick (`mover.update(..., { push })`).\n- Two to four players on one machine: `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, a mover and an input each (`game.players[i]`).\n\n**Gauntlet.** Start from the ADVENTURE slice (a `TileMap` dungeon in `threequarter` or `topdown`).\n- Up to four heroes (`game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`, `cam.frame(heroes)`), health draining by the second, food and potions, keys for doors.\n- Generators spawn monsters until destroyed; hundreds of them walk one shared `E.FlowField` toward the nearest hero (one field for all, recomputed when a hero crosses a cell), drawn as rigs with `{ every: 3 }` (kept images) to stay fast.\n\n**Frogger.** An `E.Grid` of lanes in `overhead`: the frog an `E.GridMover` that hops a cell per press; each lane a row of cars or logs moving at its own speed (`x += v * dt`, wrapping at the screen's edge). On a road a car's box kills; on the river the frog rides a log (add its speed) and drowns off one. Five homes at the top; a clock bar per life.\n\n**Sokoban.** An `E.Grid` from text rows (`#` walls, `$` crates, `.` goals, `@` you); you an `E.GridMover` with `push(cx, cy, dx, dy)` that moves a crate when the cell beyond is free; won when every goal has a crate. Keep each move's state for undo (Backspace) and a reset; count moves and pushes.\n\n**Sonic without the loops (Sonic the Hedgehog's speed on flat ground and slopes).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice: `new E.Platformer({ momentum: true })` (speed builds up and carries, slowing on the way up a slope and gathering on the way down: tiles of `kind: 'slope'`), a roll on down at speed, rings that scatter when hit (`E.Bullets` fired with `grav`) and are lost after a while, springs and long camera leads (`game.follow(hero, { lead: 60 })`). Loops and walls run round need a ground of any angle the Platformer doesn't have: leave them out.\n\n**Cinematic platformer (Prince of Persia, Flashback, Another World).** Start from the PLATFORMER slice with `new E.Platformer({ ledge: true, crouch: true })`: a step is a fixed distance, a jump a fixed arc (no control in the air), a ledge grabbed and climbed in steps, a run that needs room to stop. Screens one at a time (`game.cam.room`), each a room of the rooms module's `E.World`. Animation is the point: the Humanoid's moves at their full key poses, or a mocap clip (`docs/MOCAP.md`) for the climb and the fall; a sword fight is an `E.Fighter` pair from the fight module, in the side view.\n\n**Light guns (Time Crisis, The House of the Dead, Virtua Cop).** Start from the 3D FLIGHT slice's rails (or a 2D screen of `overhead` sprites): the camera rides an `E.Path` (`world.camera` following the rail's point), foes pop out from behind cover on a script by distance, and the player aims with the pointer: a click (a tap on a phone) at `input.pointer()` hits the nearest foe whose `world.project(x, y, z)` point lies within its size on the screen of it and that the camera sees (`world.clear` from the camera's pose); the reticle is drawn at the pointer. Reload by shooting off the screen (or a button); a pedal to take cover (a button that lowers the camera behind cover). Each enemy's shot is telegraphed by a ring that closes.\n\n**Indoor corridor shooter (Doom, GoldenEye 007) on the 3D engine.** Start from the 3D SHOOTER slice's first person: its gun, hitscan (`world.ray`), recoil and hit markers, with a level of text instead of its terrain (`level: { rows }`: corridors of walls, doors that slide or swing, `world.thing('door', ...)` with `locked: 'red'` and keys, lifts as platforms). Monsters are mobs that walk the level's flow field toward you and fire when `world.clear` says they see you; the camera is `'fixed'` with `capture: true`, turned by `camera.takeMouse()`. GoldenEye adds aiming down the sights (the overlay's fov) and guards that react to where they are shot.\n\n**Mash-ups.** The kits don't depend on each other. Some examples:\n- a platformer with Zelda rooms: `cam.room` in the side view;\n- a shooter with RPG shops: scenes plus `Menu`;\n- a brawler with bullet-hell bosses: `Bullets` with the `'ground'` plane;\n- a puzzle game with dialog cutscenes: `Dialog`.\n\n## Building a big game: lessons from Emberdeep\n`examples/emberdeep.html` is the engine's signature game: an endless Diablo-style hack-and-slash of about 2 MB of code, built from some fifty source files. Its structure (`src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`) scales to any large game.\n\n- **A spine, then content.** A small core defines:\n  - the shared state;\n  - one damage pipeline for every unit;\n  - a handful of effect verbs (projectile, nova, ground area, telegraph, strike, meteor, chain, beam, wave, pull);\n  - the hero's action model;\n  - the monster runtime;\n  - the level builder;\n  - the UI kit.\n  Content never edits the core.\n- **Registries make a language.** Every part is registered: `def('skills' | 'archetypes' | 'affixes' | 'bosses' | 'patterns' | 'itemBases' | 'powers' | 'mechanics' | 'themes' | 'layouts' | 'npcs', id, spec)`. Parts refer to each other by id and combine freely. A monster is an archetype × an element × affixes × depth scaling × a palette. A level is a layout × a theme × mechanics × a monster pool. A boss is a body × an element × patterns.\n- **An event bus instead of hooks everywhere.** Legendary powers, passive keystones, mechanics and tips listen to `hit`, `kill`, `skill`, `dodge`, `step` and `draw` events and check whether they apply. Adding a power never touches the combat code.\n- **Procedural forever.** A recipe per depth draws from the registries. Past the hand-planned depths it composes new combinations in a fixed order (every pair of mechanics, then every triple), recolors themes by depth, varies packs (elements, shared affixes, giants, swarms) and assembles bosses from a pattern library. Both sides of the power curve grow exponentially, the monsters' a little faster.\n- **New animation on the rigs.** Post-process the Humanoid's joints after `rig.update`:\n  - a dodge roll turns every joint around the hips;\n  - drinking re-solves one arm with `E.ik3`;\n  - held tools are drawn from the hand and elbow joints.\n  New creature rigs (IK-legged spiders, a path-following serpent) are posed in 3D and projected with `view.p`.\n- **Test like a player.**\n  - An autopilot drives the hero through a virtual input for balance runs and an attract-mode demo.\n  - Scripted headless playtests (`tools/ed-play.mjs`) and a smoke test over every scene and depth (`tools/ed-smoke.mjs`) keep a big game honest.\n  - A Gallery scene plays every skill and monster for visual review.\n\n## Where to find more: animation and examples\nThe engine files carry the engine; the repo (github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge) also holds a store of motion and a whole game of worked examples. Take from it rather than inventing a walk cycle or a spider from nothing, but take only what a game needs: the store's files are large (a clip set is 75-820 KB, each motion-capture subject about 2.5 MB), so search them and never read one whole. All of it runs at https://my-3d2dge.vercel.app/labs.\n\n**Motion clips** (`docs/MOCAP.md`): 325 curated clips and all 2,548 takes of the CMU motion capture database, in a readable text format of key poses (whole numbers, a legend in every file's header) that plays on any Humanoid at its own build, on either engine (the full engine also turns the face and hair with the body, for rolls and cartwheels). Every clip is free to use: Quaternius and Mesh2Motion are CC0, CMU is free for all uses.\n- **Find** a clip by what it does. The curated sets have catalogs, each clip's name with its tags and a description: `src/mocap/catalogs/quaternius.json` (88 clips: idles, walks, runs, jumps, sword, gun, shield, magic and fist attacks, dodges, hits, deaths, sitting, talking, work), `mesh2motion.json` (177: idles, attacks, emotes and gestures, dodges, climbing, sneaking, zombies, chores) and `cmu.json` (60 moments cut from motion capture: gaits, fights and kicks, dodges, falls and get-ups, chores, monsters and zombies acted by people). The motion-capture takes are in a ledger, one a line with its category, length and description: `node tools/cmu.mjs ledger kick` searches it, or grep `src/mocap/catalogs/cmu-takes.tsv`.\n- **Take** the clips you want into a small set of your own: `node tools/anim-set.mjs src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js examples/cmu-lib/CMU_10.js --clips Idle_Loop,10_01 --name MINE --out mine.js` (the curated sets are `src/mocap/sets/<set>.js`; take `NN_xx` is in `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_<NN>.js`). A clip copied by hand also needs its set's preamble (its `sources`, the bodies it was measured on), so use the tool when you can run commands.\n- **Play** it: load `src/mocap/readable.js`, `src/mocap/mocap.js` and the set after the engine, then `const lib = Mocap.load(MOCAP.MINE); Mocap.drive(rig, lib);` once, and each step `rig.mocap = lib.sample(lib.clip('Idle_Loop'), t)` before `rig.update`. `rig.mocapW` (0 to 1) fades a clip in and out over the rig's own animation, `rig.mocapMask = 'upper'` plays only the chest, head and arms over the rig's own walk, and `lib.moveAt(clip, t)` says how far a travelling clip has carried the body.\n- **Edit** it: a clip is text. Change a key pose's numbers to fix or vary it (the Mocap Lab's AI panel shows a clip as text and plays an edit).\n\n**Objects and materials** (`docs/ASSETS.md`, full engine): still objects written as a few lines of named parts (a barrel is a turned profile of oak and two iron hoops), drawn from any view and facing with `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` and made solid with `map.solid(...)`; materials (`E.MATERIALS`) dress floors, walls and platform tiles. The card has the format, the core library and the recipe: copy the nearest entry, change it, look at it with `node tools/asset-sheet.mjs mine.js#stool`, use it.\n\n**Worked examples** (`src/emberdeep/`, `src/emberdeep/DESIGN.md`): Emberdeep registers every part on one line, so one search lists them all: `grep -nE \"def\\(['\\\"](archetypes|characters|skills)\" src/emberdeep/*.js` gives the 29 monster, boss and dummy bodies (with tags: beast, spider, flying, ranged...), the 3 heroes and the skills (Codex's six come from one helper in `29-codex-skills.js`). Bodies that are not people are classes: `grep -n \"^class \" src/emberdeep/*.js` (an eight-legged crawler with IK legs, a burrowing serpent, a floating eye with a beam, Codex the living book, Dan the scythed beast on reverse-kneed legs). Perception and paired actions in play: `32-wardens.js` (a sentry's mind from `E.Suspicion` and `E.sees`, its cone from `r.sightCone`, an alarm heard through `E.hears`) and `23-executions.js` (`E.ACTIONS.takedown` on the hero and a monster at once, the target from `E.targets`). They lean on the game's helpers and shared state, so adapt them rather than paste them; `docs/CHARACTERS.md` is the recipe for a new body and what makes one read at game scale.\n\n**Starters**: `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-<genre>.html`, one vertical slice per genre (21 of them: the README's kits list, the API card's playbook), each a complete game to copy from (in the handoff package, `kits/`).\n\n**Real 3D**: `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` puts a three.js and Rapier world under a game (make the game with `bg: null`); its header is its manual, with a complete game. Its kits, `dist/kits/my-3d2dge-3d-<action|platformer|shooter|horror|racing|flight|crawler>.html`, are served, not opened from a disk (they load `vendor/`); the handoff package leaves them out. `docs/ENGINE-3D.md` says how it fits the engine.\n\n## Performance\n- **Rigs:** projection costs microseconds. The outline composite is the expensive part, so crowds should use `outline: false`.\n- **Walls, floors and PlatformMap chunks** are baked once per view.\n- **Canvas lighting** scans pixels inside each light, so keep radii sensible or use GPU lighting.\n- **Particles** are capped at 900 (`game.particles.max`) and bullets at 800 per pool.\n- **Crowd separation:** use `E.SpatialHash`, not all-pairs checks.\n- The stress test (my-3d2dge.vercel.app/stress-test) benchmarks a device and prints a copyable report.\n\n## Checklist before handing a game back\n- One IIFE starting with `const E = My3D2dge`, holding only your game (no leftover starter menu or slices): between `GAME START` and `GAME END` in a single file, `game/game.js` in the handoff package. No libraries or network requests.\n- A title scene, a gameplay scene with `pausable: true`, and a game-over or win path back to the title.\n- No pause menu, settings screen, key rebinding or full-screen code of your own: they're standard. Your settings, names and menu items go in `new E.Game({ menu: {...} })`; prompts come from `game.input.label(action)`.","numLines":125,"startLine":535,"totalLines":665}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KNva93V9GX3ugyPCYghfmY","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
