{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFWPuRAvrjThwhQctFF7Mb","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Olympiyikes!\n2\t\n3\t**Tagline:** *Faster. Higher. Yikes.*\n4\t**One line:** the complete Summer *and* Winter Games, held in the same fortnight in one twin-city host, played as\n5\tan over-the-top arcade multi-event: 12 events, 8 nations, ceremonies, a rival, two hyperventilating commentators,\n6\t1–4 players on the couch and AI Hub ghost heats online.\n7\t\n8\t> **How the title was picked (TASK.md rule):** 50 riffs on \"Olympic Games\" were brainstormed silently, then\n9\t> `secrets.randbelow(50)` (Python, OS CSPRNG) returned **16 → \"Olympiyikes!\"**. Do not re-roll it. Its file-name\n10\t> form is `Olympiyikes`; the SOURCE token is `OlympicGames`.\n11\t\n12\t---\n13\t\n14\t## 0. Read this first (ground rules for the builders)\n15\t\n16\t- **Inputs:** `TASK.md` (requirements) + this plan. If they disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan is silent, follow\n17\t  the engine's own docs (`API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`) and the plan's spirit. Everything listed here ships: **no TODOs,\n18\t  placeholders, locked \"coming soon\" slots or stubbed features.**\n19\t- **Names (with this TASK.md):** dev folder `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/`, deliverable\n20\t  `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837.html` in the root (beside TASK.md), and\n21\t  `exit-interview.json` in the root. If your TASK.md's name pattern carries a different model/date, use your pattern\n22\t  with `Olympiyikes` and `OlympicGames`.\n23\t- **Shared machine:** read only the files you need (other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes).\n24\t  Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n25\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from your dev folder, read the port it prints, and confirm the served page\n26\t  is *your* game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`,\n27\t  `fuser -k`. During planning `/tmp` was 100% full: if npm/Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp`.\n28\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge handoff package, **v0.85.2** at planning time\n29\t  (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`). We own our copy and may change it (§4.6).\n30\t- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`).\n31\t- **No subagents/workflows to debate, review, judge or audit** (TASK.md). Tests are scripts.\n32\t- **Units used in this plan:** world units (u) are the engine's (one tile = 16 u, z is up). Distances shown to the\n33\t  player are meters; each event says its scale (default **M = 12 u per meter**). Times in seconds unless marked ms.\n34\t  The engine runs fixed steps of 1/120 s.\n35\t\n36\t### 0.1 Fetch what you build on (once, inside the dev folder)\n37\t\n38\t```sh\n39\tD=Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837 && mkdir -p $D/{engine,src,tests,tools,vendor/hub,.tmp} && cd $D\n40\tgit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\n41\tgit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\n42\tcp vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js          # our engine copy (we edit this one)\n43\tfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do\n44\t  curl -fsSL -o vendor/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n45\t(cd vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff && export TMPDIR=\"$(cd ../../../.. && pwd)/.tmp\" && npm install && npx playwright install chromium)\n46\t```\n47\t\n48\tRead whole: `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API.md`, `README.md`, `kits/README.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`,\n49\t`vendor/hub/README.md`. Read as references (copy patterns, not whole files): `kits/sports.js` (hockey), `kits/road.js`\n50\t(downhill/bobsled), `kits/rhythm.js` (figure skating), `kits/pinball.js` + `modules/physics.js` (curling),\n51\t`kits/kart.js` (drawing a rig through a custom view), `modules/arcade.js` (record tables, initials), `game/test.mjs`\n52\tand `kits/test.mjs` (headless harness). Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 1. The game\n57\t\n58\t### 1.1 Pitch\n59\t\n60\tFor the first time in history the Summer and Winter Games share one fortnight and one host: **Mount Glory–Sunny Bay**,\n61\twhere a ski jump towers over the beach and the swimming pool shares a wall with the ice rink. Eight fictional nations\n62\tsend their stars. You pick a nation and its athlete, light the cauldron, and chase gold across **12 events** that\n63\talternate summer and winter, while defending champion **Max Overdrive of Turbonia** insists, loudly, that it is all\n64\tpointless because he will win. Everything is broadcast like the real thing (parade of nations, \"Up Close & Personal\"\n65\tprofiles, photo finishes, medal ceremonies with anthems, a medal table) and played like an arcade cabinet (mashing,\n66\ttiming, aiming, steering, rhythm, physics; a HYPE meter that sets you on fire; world records that explode into\n67\tfireworks). When it goes wrong, it goes *gloriously* wrong: false starts, belly flops, ski-jump tumbles, bobsleds on\n68\ttheir roof. That's an **OLYMPIYIKES!**, and the game celebrates it.\n69\t\n70\t### 1.2 Pillars\n71\t\n72\t1. **Ten seconds to learn, a lifetime to perfect.** Each event teaches one verb (mash, time, aim, steer, keep the beat,\n73\t   sweep) on one card, and has a perfect-play ceiling with world records to chase.\n74\t2. **Broadcast spectacle.** Ceremonies, commentators, crowd, jumbotron, slow-mo, photo finishes, anthems, records.\n75\t3. **Failure is a show.** Every event has at least one comic \"Yikes\" moment that is funny, fast to recover from, and\n76\t   even pays a little hype (§5.8).\n77\t4. **Both seasons.** 6 summer + 6 winter events on alternating days; each venue has its own look, light and music.\n78\t5. **Couch first, online too.** 1–4 local players in every mode; AI Hub ghost heats when the hub launches the game.\n79\t\n80\t### 1.3 Genre and perspective (decisions)\n81\t\n82\t- **Genre:** arcade multi-event sports (Track & Field / Winter Games lineage) with a short campaign, local party\n83\t  play and online ghost heats. Each event is a compact mini-game built on one shared event framework.\n84\t- **Perspective:** per event, all on the engine's native 2D views (pixel art at `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`):\n85\t  - **2.5D side-on lanes** (`'brawler'` view, pitch 25): 100m Dash, Javelin, 50m Freestyle, Weightlifting.\n86\t  - **Pure side view** (`'side'`): 10m Diving, Ski Jump, the archer's inset.\n87\t  - **Target close-up overlay:** Archery's main view.\n88\t  - **Pseudo-3D behind the athlete** (the `road` module, `E.Road`): Downhill, Bobsled.\n89\t  - **Three-quarter** (`'threequarter'`): Figure Skating, Ice Hockey, ceremonies' podium shots use `'brawler'`.\n90\t  - **Top-down** (`'topdown'`): Curling.\n91\t  Rationale: each event gets the camera its classic arcade ancestors used, and the engine has a native, tested view\n92\t  for every one of them.\n93\t\n94\t### 1.4 Core loop\n95\t\n96\tPick nation → (ceremony) → **day card** (who, where, a human-interest segment) → **event card** (one screen: goal and\n97\tcontrols with button glyphs) → **compete** (live vs CPU rivals and friends) → **results** (records, qualifying) →\n98\t**medal ceremony** → **medal table** (story beat) → next day. 12 days, then the closing ceremony and an ending that\n99\tdepends on your nation's rank.\n100\t\n101\t### 1.5 Modes (main menu, in this order)\n102\t\n103\t| Item | What it is |\n104\t|---|---|\n105\t| CONTINUE GAMES | only when an autosave exists: resumes The Games at the next day |\n106\t| THE GAMES | the campaign: opening ceremony, 12 days (all 12 events), story beats, halftime report, closing ceremony, ending. 1–4 players |\n107\t| QUICK GAMES | 4 events (playlists: *Track & Pool*, *Snow & Ice*, *Greatest Hits*, *Random 4*) with mini ceremonies; 1–4 players, party points |\n108\t| SINGLE EVENT | any of the 12 events, vs CPU or practice (no rivals); 1–4 players |\n109\t| RECORDS | world records and top-5 tables per event (initials), the Laurel cabinet, the Medal cabinet |\n110\t| HOW TO PLAY | Basics (mash, timing, hype & turbo, yikes) + every event's card |\n111\t| SETTINGS | the engine's settings screen plus the game's own (§2.5) |\n112\t| ABOUT & CREDITS | Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, credits, engine changes, controls |\n113\t\n114\tOnline play is not a menu item: it starts only when the AI Hub launches the page (§12.2).\n115\t\n116\t---\n117\t\n118\t## 2. Controls and input (controller-first, keyboard and mouse native)\n119\t\n120\t### 2.1 The action map (custom preset `OLY_KEYS`, replaces the engine presets for the whole game)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f2086767-996d-4677-894d-e4b97c87d2db","uuid":"faa03bfa-5c26-43ae-8510-608e31d48441","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:33:10.835Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174837__x7ac903/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Olympiyikes!\n\n**Tagline:** *Faster. Higher. Yikes.*\n**One line:** the complete Summer *and* Winter Games, held in the same fortnight in one twin-city host, played as\nan over-the-top arcade multi-event: 12 events, 8 nations, ceremonies, a rival, two hyperventilating commentators,\n1–4 players on the couch and AI Hub ghost heats online.\n\n> **How the title was picked (TASK.md rule):** 50 riffs on \"Olympic Games\" were brainstormed silently, then\n> `secrets.randbelow(50)` (Python, OS CSPRNG) returned **16 → \"Olympiyikes!\"**. Do not re-roll it. Its file-name\n> form is `Olympiyikes`; the SOURCE token is `OlympicGames`.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Read this first (ground rules for the builders)\n\n- **Inputs:** `TASK.md` (requirements) + this plan. If they disagree, TASK.md wins. Where this plan is silent, follow\n  the engine's own docs (`API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`) and the plan's spirit. Everything listed here ships: **no TODOs,\n  placeholders, locked \"coming soon\" slots or stubbed features.**\n- **Names (with this TASK.md):** dev folder `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837/`, deliverable\n  `Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837.html` in the root (beside TASK.md), and\n  `exit-interview.json` in the root. If your TASK.md's name pattern carries a different model/date, use your pattern\n  with `Olympiyikes` and `OlympicGames`.\n- **Shared machine:** read only the files you need (other agents share the parent directories, ports and processes).\n  Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), run\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from your dev folder, read the port it prints, and confirm the served page\n  is *your* game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`,\n  `fuser -k`. During planning `/tmp` was 100% full: if npm/Playwright fail with ENOSPC, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp`.\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge handoff package, **v0.85.2** at planning time\n  (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`). We own our copy and may change it (§4.6).\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client **1.4.0** (`https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`).\n- **No subagents/workflows to debate, review, judge or audit** (TASK.md). Tests are scripts.\n- **Units used in this plan:** world units (u) are the engine's (one tile = 16 u, z is up). Distances shown to the\n  player are meters; each event says its scale (default **M = 12 u per meter**). Times in seconds unless marked ms.\n  The engine runs fixed steps of 1/120 s.\n\n### 0.1 Fetch what you build on (once, inside the dev folder)\n\n```sh\nD=Olympiyikes-OlympicGames-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174837 && mkdir -p $D/{engine,src,tests,tools,vendor/hub,.tmp} && cd $D\ngit clone --depth 1 --filter=blob:none --sparse https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge.git vendor/my-3d2dge\ngit -C vendor/my-3d2dge sparse-checkout set dist/handoff\ncp vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/my-3d2dge.js engine/my-3d2dge.js          # our engine copy (we edit this one)\nfor f in session.js harness.html bridge.js round.js README.md; do\n  curl -fsSL -o vendor/hub/$f https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/$f; done\n(cd vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff && export TMPDIR=\"$(cd ../../../.. && pwd)/.tmp\" && npm install && npx playwright install chromium)\n```\n\nRead whole: `vendor/my-3d2dge/dist/handoff/API.md`, `README.md`, `kits/README.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`,\n`vendor/hub/README.md`. Read as references (copy patterns, not whole files): `kits/sports.js` (hockey), `kits/road.js`\n(downhill/bobsled), `kits/rhythm.js` (figure skating), `kits/pinball.js` + `modules/physics.js` (curling),\n`kits/kart.js` (drawing a rig through a custom view), `modules/arcade.js` (record tables, initials), `game/test.mjs`\nand `kits/test.mjs` (headless harness). Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names; don't read it whole.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n### 1.1 Pitch\n\nFor the first time in history the Summer and Winter Games share one fortnight and one host: **Mount Glory–Sunny Bay**,\nwhere a ski jump towers over the beach and the swimming pool shares a wall with the ice rink. Eight fictional nations\nsend their stars. You pick a nation and its athlete, light the cauldron, and chase gold across **12 events** that\nalternate summer and winter, while defending champion **Max Overdrive of Turbonia** insists, loudly, that it is all\npointless because he will win. Everything is broadcast like the real thing (parade of nations, \"Up Close & Personal\"\nprofiles, photo finishes, medal ceremonies with anthems, a medal table) and played like an arcade cabinet (mashing,\ntiming, aiming, steering, rhythm, physics; a HYPE meter that sets you on fire; world records that explode into\nfireworks). When it goes wrong, it goes *gloriously* wrong: false starts, belly flops, ski-jump tumbles, bobsleds on\ntheir roof. That's an **OLYMPIYIKES!**, and the game celebrates it.\n\n### 1.2 Pillars\n\n1. **Ten seconds to learn, a lifetime to perfect.** Each event teaches one verb (mash, time, aim, steer, keep the beat,\n   sweep) on one card, and has a perfect-play ceiling with world records to chase.\n2. **Broadcast spectacle.** Ceremonies, commentators, crowd, jumbotron, slow-mo, photo finishes, anthems, records.\n3. **Failure is a show.** Every event has at least one comic \"Yikes\" moment that is funny, fast to recover from, and\n   even pays a little hype (§5.8).\n4. **Both seasons.** 6 summer + 6 winter events on alternating days; each venue has its own look, light and music.\n5. **Couch first, online too.** 1–4 local players in every mode; AI Hub ghost heats when the hub launches the game.\n\n### 1.3 Genre and perspective (decisions)\n\n- **Genre:** arcade multi-event sports (Track & Field / Winter Games lineage) with a short campaign, local party\n  play and online ghost heats. Each event is a compact mini-game built on one shared event framework.\n- **Perspective:** per event, all on the engine's native 2D views (pixel art at `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`):\n  - **2.5D side-on lanes** (`'brawler'` view, pitch 25): 100m Dash, Javelin, 50m Freestyle, Weightlifting.\n  - **Pure side view** (`'side'`): 10m Diving, Ski Jump, the archer's inset.\n  - **Target close-up overlay:** Archery's main view.\n  - **Pseudo-3D behind the athlete** (the `road` module, `E.Road`): Downhill, Bobsled.\n  - **Three-quarter** (`'threequarter'`): Figure Skating, Ice Hockey, ceremonies' podium shots use `'brawler'`.\n  - **Top-down** (`'topdown'`): Curling.\n  Rationale: each event gets the camera its classic arcade ancestors used, and the engine has a native, tested view\n  for every one of them.\n\n### 1.4 Core loop\n\nPick nation → (ceremony) → **day card** (who, where, a human-interest segment) → **event card** (one screen: goal and\ncontrols with button glyphs) → **compete** (live vs CPU rivals and friends) → **results** (records, qualifying) →\n**medal ceremony** → **medal table** (story beat) → next day. 12 days, then the closing ceremony and an ending that\ndepends on your nation's rank.\n\n### 1.5 Modes (main menu, in this order)\n\n| Item | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| CONTINUE GAMES | only when an autosave exists: resumes The Games at the next day |\n| THE GAMES | the campaign: opening ceremony, 12 days (all 12 events), story beats, halftime report, closing ceremony, ending. 1–4 players |\n| QUICK GAMES | 4 events (playlists: *Track & Pool*, *Snow & Ice*, *Greatest Hits*, *Random 4*) with mini ceremonies; 1–4 players, party points |\n| SINGLE EVENT | any of the 12 events, vs CPU or practice (no rivals); 1–4 players |\n| RECORDS | world records and top-5 tables per event (initials), the Laurel cabinet, the Medal cabinet |\n| HOW TO PLAY | Basics (mash, timing, hype & turbo, yikes) + every event's card |\n| SETTINGS | the engine's settings screen plus the game's own (§2.5) |\n| ABOUT & CREDITS | Source Information, Testing/Engine/Prompt Feedback, credits, engine changes, controls |\n\nOnline play is not a menu item: it starts only when the AI Hub launches the page (§12.2).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Controls and input (controller-first, keyboard and mouse native)\n\n### 2.1 The action map (custom preset `OLY_KEYS`, replaces the engine presets for the whole game)","numLines":120,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1715}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AFWPuRAvrjThwhQctFF7Mb","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
